-<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/sell/net-proceeds/#faq","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"How much does it cost to sell a house in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Plan for the agent commission, deed stamps of 1.85 dollars per 500 dollars of price, an attorney closing fee, a title policy, any HOA transfer fees, your prorated property taxes, and whatever you agree to credit the buyer. On most sales the commission is the largest line and deed stamps are second."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What are deed stamps in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A state and county recording fee charged when the deed transfers: 1.85 dollars per 500 dollars of the sale price, about 0.37 percent. On a 300,000 dollar sale that is 1,110 dollars. The seller pays it by law and by custom."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does the seller need an attorney in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. South Carolina requires a licensed attorney to conduct real estate closings, so the attorney fee is part of every sale, agent-listed or not."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I pay capital gains tax when I sell?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Sometimes. A primary home usually gets a large federal exclusion, and rentals and second homes are taxed differently. Out-of-state sellers also face South Carolina withholding at closing. Our capital gains page walks through all of it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is a seller net sheet?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A line-by-line estimate of what you walk away with: price minus payoff, commission, deed stamps, attorney and title fees, prorations, and credits. We prepare one free for your exact situation on request."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is the commission always 6 percent?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. Commissions are negotiable and vary by brokerage and by listing. The calculator on this page uses an editable example so you can model any rate."}}]}</script>
